<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Monnet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Monnet was a French economist and diplomat regarded as a founder of European integration. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he headed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s General Planning Commission, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monnet-plan">Monnet Plan</a> guided reconstruction and modernisation from 1946. He devised the Schuman Declaration of 1950 and became the first president of the High Authority of the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> Community in 1952.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-monnet.jpg" alt="Jean Monnet" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Lambiotte / European Commission · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Monnet_1967.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
